---
// Second informational page. Targets "what to do if you bought from a scam
// website" and the "can i get money back if i got scammed" cluster, both found
// by `npm run demand -- info`.
//
// This page is deliberately NOT data-driven — the corpus measures storefronts,
// not outcomes, and dressing recovery advice in our statistics would be using
// numbers that do not bear on the question. What it must be is accurate and
// time-aware, because the single thing that decides whether someone gets their
// money back is how fast they act.
//
// Money-back rules differ by country, so this covers the four markets the site
// targets and says plainly where the reader must check their own provider.
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';

const title = 'What to Do if You Bought From a Scam Website';
const description =
  'Practical steps to try to get your money back after ordering from a fraudulent store — ' +
  'what your payment method allows, the deadlines that apply, and where to report it.';

const jsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'HowTo',
  name: title,
  description,
  step: [
    { '@type': 'HowToStep', name: 'Stop further payments', text: 'Cancel any subscription or saved payment authorisation with the merchant immediately.' },
    { '@type': 'HowToStep', name: 'Collect your evidence', text: 'Save the order confirmation, receipts, listing pages, and all correspondence before the site changes or disappears.' },
    { '@type': 'HowToStep', name: 'Contact your payment provider', text: 'Open a dispute or chargeback with the card issuer or payment platform. Time limits apply and are often counted from the expected delivery date.' },
    { '@type': 'HowToStep', name: 'Report it', text: 'File with your national fraud reporting body so the merchant is on record.' },
  ],
};
---
<BaseLayout title={`${title} — CheckIfLegit`} description={description}>
  <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(jsonLd)} />

  <p class="eyebrow"><a href="/guides/">← Guides</a></p>
  <h1>{title}</h1>

  <p class="lede">
    The thing that most decides whether you get your money back is how quickly you start.
    Dispute rights run on deadlines, and several of them are counted from the order date
    rather than from the day you realised something was wrong.
  </p>

  <h2>1. Stop anything still running</h2>
  <p>
    Cancel any subscription, saved card authorisation or recurring payment with the
    merchant before you do anything else. If you handed over card details on a site you now
    distrust, treat the card as compromised and ask your bank to reissue it — a fraudulent
    merchant with working card details is a continuing problem, not a closed one.
  </p>

  <h2>2. Save the evidence now, not later</h2>
  <p>
    Fraudulent storefronts disappear, and so does the evidence. Before you contact anyone,
    capture:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>The order confirmation and any receipt or payment reference</li>
    <li>Screenshots of the product listing, the price, and any delivery promise</li>
    <li>Every email or chat message, including the ones that went unanswered</li>
    <li>The URL, and a copy of any tracking number you were given</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    A dispute is much stronger when you can show what you were promised and when. If the
    site is already gone, check whether the Internet Archive captured the listing.
  </p>

  <h2>3. Go to your payment provider — this is where the money is</h2>
  <p>
    Contacting the merchant again is usually the least productive step. Your leverage is
    with whoever moved the money.
  </p>

  <h3>Credit card</h3>
  <p>
    The strongest position. Card schemes let you raise a chargeback for goods that never
    arrived or were materially not as described. In the <strong>US</strong>, the Fair
    Credit Billing Act generally gives you 60 days from the statement showing the charge,
    though card networks often allow longer for non-delivery. In the <strong>UK</strong>,
    chargeback is typically available for around 120 days, and Section 75 of the Consumer
    Credit Act can make the card provider jointly liable for credit-card purchases roughly
    between £100 and £30,000 — which is a stronger and separate right worth naming
    explicitly when you call. Deadlines are often counted from the <em>expected delivery
    date</em>, so say so if delivery was promised for a later date.
  </p>

  <h3>Debit card</h3>
  <p>
    Weaker than credit but not nothing — Visa and Mastercard chargeback rules generally
    still apply. What you usually lose is the statutory joint-liability protection that
    credit purchases carry. Ask your bank specifically for a chargeback, not just to
    “report fraud”, which can route you into a slower process.
  </p>

  <h3>PayPal and similar platforms</h3>
  <p>
    PayPal Buyer Protection generally allows a dispute within 180 days for items that did
    not arrive or were significantly not as described. Open it inside the platform rather
    than by email so it is formally logged.
  </p>

  <h3>Bank transfer, crypto, or gift cards</h3>
  <p>
    Be prepared for a poor outcome: these are chosen by fraudulent merchants precisely
    because they are hard to reverse. Contact your bank immediately anyway — UK banks
    operate a reimbursement scheme for authorised push payment fraud, and some banks
    elsewhere will attempt a recall if you act within hours. Report it regardless, because
    the record matters even when the money does not come back.
  </p>

  <h2>4. Report it</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>US</strong> — the FTC at reportfraud.ftc.gov, and the FBI's IC3 at ic3.gov</li>
    <li><strong>UK</strong> — Action Fraud, or Police Scotland if you are in Scotland</li>
    <li><strong>Canada</strong> — the Canadian Anti-Fraud Centre</li>
    <li><strong>Australia</strong> — Scamwatch, run by the ACCC</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Reporting rarely recovers your own money directly. It matters because it is how
    repeated merchants get identified, and because a filed report sometimes strengthens a
    bank dispute.
  </p>

  <h2>5. Expect the pressure tactics</h2>
  <p>
    Two things happen often enough to warn about. Some merchants offer a partial refund
    — 30% or so — in exchange for closing the dispute or removing a review; accepting can
    forfeit the fuller claim. And being scammed once puts you on lists sold to others, so
    treat any unsolicited “recovery service” offering to retrieve your money for an upfront
    fee as a second attempt on your money.
  </p>

  <h2>Before the next order</h2>
  <p>
    The check that would most often have caught it is looking at what the page's code does
    rather than what it says — fabricated urgency and purchase notices are implemented in
    the browser and visible to anyone who looks. We cover how in
    <a href="/guides/how-to-check-if-a-website-is-real">how to check if a website is real</a>.
  </p>

  <p class="muted small">
    This is general information, not legal or financial advice, and consumer protection
    rules vary by country and change over time. Confirm the current deadlines and rights
    with your own bank, card issuer or national consumer body — they are the ones who
    decide your case.
  </p>
</BaseLayout>
